  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 313 AM EDT Sun Aug 16 2026\n\nEarly morning KMQT radar returns show scattered rain showers over \nmainly the central UP around 06Z, though no lightning has yet been \nobserved. Per RAP analysis, this is ahead of a 1011mb surface trough \nover mainly the west half of northern Ontario supported by an upper \ntrough over the Ontario/Manitoba line. Elsewhere at 500mb, a closed \nhigh is centered over the ArkLaTex region with multiple shortwaves \nrotating around the top of the high. Troughs are also present over \nBritish Columbia and off the New England coast. Throughout the \nremainder of the weekend, the southern high and Pacific trough will \nmake no meaningful progress, but the troughs off the East Coast and \nover central Canada will progress, leaving the UP in a stable, \nnorthwesterly flow pattern which will keep dry weather in the \nforecast from this afternoon (following the departure of showers to \nthe east) to Monday evening. High temperatures remain seasonable and \ncomfortable through Monday with highs only exceeding 80 degrees in \nthe south-central. Tonight into Monday morning, lows fall to around \nthe 50 degree mark for the interior and around the 60 degree mark at \nthe Great Lakes shores.\n\nToday through Monday afternoon, a 500mb trough will deepen and \nbecome a closed low as it swings along the Hudson Bay shoreline from \nNunavut to Manitoba. Falling 500mb heights ahead of this closed\nlow will lead to the formation of a surface trough that will \nonce again bring rain shower chances to the UP around 00Z \nTuesday. CAMs show some convective activity in Minnesota and \nnorthwest Ontario ongoing at 00Z Tuesday, though if convection \nsurvives crossing the lake and reaching the UP, it will be \nmoving into stable air (HREF mean MUCAPE struggles to exceed 100\nJ/kg), though it will be a shear-rich environment as HREF mean \n0-3km SRH balloons to 100-200 and deep speed shear is present \n(including the presence of a 100+kt jet at 250mb). Model \nsoundings show meager lapse rates but plentiful deep moisture \n(LREF mean PWAT grows to about 1.25 inches, around the 75th \npercentile of climatology). Thus, if any thunderstorms can get \ngoing, some heavy rain rates could be observed, but severe \nthunderstorms seem unlikely given the timing of the showers not \nlining up well with diurnal destabilization. Unfortunately for \nthe areas of growing drought for the UP, this rainfall does not \nlook to be widespread, as LREF probabilities of 0.1 inch of rain\nby 00Z Wednesday are 25-33% for the west half and 35-55% for \nthe east half. \n\nAnother dry spell then kicks off early Wednesday morning through at \nleast Thursday evening. Models diverge significantly for the late \nweek period, though the passage of another deep trough from the \nnorthwest looks to be the next mechanism for bringing shower and \nthunderstorm chances to the UP late this week into next weekend. \nWith the continued background northwesterly flow aloft pattern, \ntemperatures generally remain seasonal with highs in the 70s and \nlows in the 50s.",
